#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055979 :tutorial/content "To find the zeros of a rational function, we first need to factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the numerator equal to zero and solve.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{x-3}{x^2-25}.$ Factoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x-3}{(x+5)(x-5)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors to cancel, so we set the numerator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to $f(x)=0$ is $x=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ba9f3296-27d6-5ebc-93b3-bfd93d9384c8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3380 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
